th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/static-game-of-complete-information.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/static-game-of-complete-information.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_static_game_of_complete_information_c5523ecd_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In a static game player 1 chooses $T$ or $B$ and player 2 chooses $L$ or $R$. Payoffs $(v_1,v_2)$ are: $(T,L)$: $(3,1)$; $(T,R)$: $(0,2)$; $(B,L)$: $(2,4)$; $(B,R)$: $(1,0)$. What is $v_2(B,L)$?","options":["$4$","$2$","$1$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a2a0d87685a2a2162bea4c66","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_static_game_of_complete_information_c5523ecd_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does a static game of complete information require the payoff functions to be common knowledge rather than merely known by every player?","options":["So that each player knows in advance which actions the other players will choose.","Because \"every player knows\" already implies all higher levels, so the two coincide.","Because predicting others' choices depends on what each knows the others know.","So that payoffs are announced publicly, which is what makes the choices simultaneous."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73785345c886e9fa6bd89544","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_static_game_of_complete_information_c5523ecd_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Two people choose simultaneously; person 1's payoff depends only on person 1's action and person 2's payoff depends only on person 2's action, all common knowledge. Why is this not a genuine strategic environment?","options":["It is one, since choices are simultaneous and all payoffs are common knowledge.","Neither outcome depends on the other's choice, so each person faces a separate decision problem.","It is not, because a strategic environment requires both payoffs to be identical.","It is not, because a strategic environment requires at least three players."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5b357b9b5f11f26f349a3a2","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}